#######################################################
# CSC3050 Practice Bomb Lab
# Directions to Students
#######################################################

****************
# 1. Overview
****************

The nefarious Dr. Lethal is planning to spread his binary bombs everywhere
on our machines! A binary bomb is a program that consists of a sequence of 
phases. Each phase expects you to type a particular string on stdin. If you 
type the correct string, the phase would be defused! And if you defuse all
the phases, Dr. Lethal's atrocious scheme would be shattered! However, if 
you enter even just one incorrect string, the bomb would go off!!!

But there's also good news! The CSC3050 TA team decides to prepare you for 
this dire task. In this practice bomb lab, we will provide you with 2 (two) 
simple but sufficient phases to let you get the hang of it! Of course, this
practice lab would NOT be graded, and you can do whatever you want with the
bomb without receiving any penalties :p

You can also completely ignore this practice lab if you are confident enough
that you can stand up to Dr. Lethal on your own... But keep in mind, DO NOT
come to the TA team wailing like a crybaby if you find yourself stuck deep in
Dr. Lethal's quagmire! A man shall bear his own consequences!


****************
# 2. Files
****************

This practice lab consists of the following file structure:

bomblab_prep/
  |-
  |---bomb                  // the executable file to defuse
  |---bomb.asm              // full RISC-V disassembly of bomb
  |---bomb.c                // main function of the bomb in c
  |-
  |---README
  |-
  |---src/                  // The rest of the source code in c
       |-
       |---phases.c         // c code of phase_1 and phase_2
       |---phases.h         // header of phases.c
       |-
       |---solution.txt     // the solution of the practice lab
       |-
       |---support.c        // useful functions invoked
       |---support.h        // header of support.c


# !!! WARNING !!!: 
It is STRONGLY advised NOT to look up the source code and the solution in
src.tar before fully trying out the bomb!!! Once the logic of the program is
clear to you, debugging the assembly will be like cheating. You have to train
yourself to debug without any source code, otherwise this lab is meaningless.
      (Auctually I zipped the directory up just for this purpose)
Note that everything in src.tar will not be given to you in the real bomblab.
All you'll be seeing would be bomb, bomb.c, and README. You can generate the
bomb.asm disassembly file yourself using objdump in your RISC-V environment.


****************
# 3. Environment
****************

Both this practice bomb and the real bomb will be run in the provided QEMU
environment in the form of a Docker container (credits to USTF Vincent Janssen). 
To set it up, simply pull the container image from ghcr.io/janssen-v/rvemu 
and follow the instructions on the Github repository: 
https://github.com/janssen-v/rvemu

To move the files into the environment, you can use SCP to do so. An example 
command to do so is shown below:

scp bomb.tar -p 2222 student@localhost:~

To untar the bomb, use the command below:

tar -xvf bomb.tar

To debug and defuse the bomb, a GNU Debugger (gdb) is strongly recommended
and ready to use in your environment. It is strongly recommended to look up gdb 
manuals online before this tool is officially taught to you in our tutorials.
To start debugging, type the following command and enjoy your trip!

gdb ./bomb
